PART 3 — WORLD EVENTS SYSTEM
This is the most important technical component.
3.1 Central Event Architecture
Create a central World Events System.
Every major game action generates a structured event.
The event system distributes relevant information to the different applications.
Example:
RNT wins an international tournament.
The system generates:
TOURNAMENT_COMPLETED
The event is consumed by:
* Twitter: Match discussions and reactions.
* News: Tournament coverage.
* Instagram: Organisation victory post.
* YouTube: Analysis and highlights.
* Bank: Prize money transaction.
* Team Stocks: Organisation valuation changes.
* Messenger: Team congratulatory messages.
One event should be capable of generating multiple consequences.
3.2 Event Types
Initial event catalogue:
Competitive Events
* MATCH_STARTED
* MATCH_COMPLETED
* TOURNAMENT_STARTED
* TOURNAMENT_COMPLETED
* TEAM_QUALIFIED
* TEAM_ELIMINATED
* PLAYER_PERFORMANCE_RECORDED
Organisation Events
* ORGANISATION_CREATED
* PLAYER_SIGNED
* PLAYER_RELEASED
* PLAYER_TRANSFERRED
* COACH_HIRED
* SPONSORSHIP_SIGNED
* ORGANISATION_BANKRUPTCY
Player Events
* PLAYER_PROMOTED
* PLAYER_BENCHED
* PLAYER_CONTRACT_EXPIRED
* PLAYER_REPUTATION_CHANGED
* PLAYER_RETIREMENT
Community Events
* ORGANISATION_TRENDING
* PLAYER_TRENDING
* CONTROVERSY_CREATED
* RIVALRY_INTENSIFIED
* SOCIAL_POST_PUBLISHED
Economic Events
* SALARY_PAID
* SPONSORSHIP_PAYMENT_RECEIVED
* TOURNAMENT_PRIZE_RECEIVED
* STOCK_PRICE_UPDATED
* VIDEO_REVENUE_RECEIVED
3.3 TypeScript Event Structure
Create:
src/engine/events/types.ts
export type WorldEventType =
  | "MATCH_COMPLETED"
  | "TOURNAMENT_COMPLETED"
  | "PLAYER_SIGNED"
  | "PLAYER_TRANSFERRED"
  | "PLAYER_BENCHED"
  | "SPONSORSHIP_SIGNED"
  | "CONTROVERSY_CREATED"
  | "PLAYER_TRENDING"
  | "SALARY_PAID"
  | "STOCK_PRICE_UPDATED";

export interface WorldEvent {
  id: string;
  type: WorldEventType;
  timestamp: number;
  gameDay: number;

  actorId?: string;
  targetId?: string;

  title: string;
  description: string;

  payload: Record<string, unknown>;

  importance: "LOW" | "MEDIUM" | "HIGH";
}
Use typed event-specific payloads as the system expands.
3.4 Event Processing
Recommended flow:
GAME ACTION ↓ WORLD EVENT CREATED ↓ EVENT VALIDATION ↓ WORLD EVENT PROCESSOR ↓ APPLICATION LISTENERS ↓ APPLICATION CONTENT GENERATED ↓ GAME STATE UPDATED
Important requirements:
1. An event must not generate duplicate transactions.
2. Events must preserve their original game date.
3. Applications must consume the same underlying event.
4. Events must be deterministic when generated from the same game state and random seed.
5. Event processing must not create infinite event loops.
6. Important events should remain available in the historical event log.
 
